This is very interesting; this is a similar rhetorical point to "why does
the game kill you when you run out of HP", but being used to drive in the
opposite direction.

What happens to the Tournament in this hypothetical case?

>   1) If the game has an abusable feature, there's nobody holding a
>      gun to your head making you abuse it. You could always -- and
>      I know this is a radical idea, bear with me -- just not do that
>      if the game is more fun for you without.

I think everyone knows that.

Part of the problem, beyond the damage caused to multiplayer setups, is
that often these abusable techniques are blocking the development of
interesting gameplay. #ascend is one thing; you either use it or you
don't. However, polypiling - for example - became more interesting when it
was cut back. Do I turn junk potions into water, or polypile them for a
lesser number of potentially useful potions? If polypiling doesn't destroy
objects, that choice is meaningless.

>   3) Maybe we could get past protecting the sacred temple of the
>      ascended from the threat that a newb might stumble in and wreck
>      all our hard-earned bragging rights.

Again, this is a persecution fantasy from you-know-who. Thousands of
people have ascended; anyone reasonably intelligent will do so with
persistence and caution; and there are many players who, thanks to the
Tournament, I know as an absolute fact are enormously better at the game
than I am. There's not much in the way of bragging rights there.
